==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*gʷerH-}}
From {{bor|en|la|grātuītus||free}}, from {{der|en|la|grātia||favor}}, {{m|la|grātus||showing favor}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɡɹəˈtjuː.ɪt.əs/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɡɹəˈtu.ɪt.əs/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-gratuitous.ogg|a=US}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Given freely; [[unearned]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 January 11|author=Stephen Roberts|title=Bradshaw's Britain: castles and cathedrals|journal=RAIL|issue=974|page=56|text=Also mentioned is the Rev. T. Stock, who has a tablet in St John's church [Gloucester] and "who with Raikes established the four original Sunday schools in this parish ... in 1780. From this small beginning sprung that '''gratuitous''' system of Christian instruction which has covered the face of England and Wales with schools."}}
# [[unjustified|Unjustified]] or [[unnecessary]]; not [[call for|called for]] by the circumstances.
#: {{syn|en|uncalled-for}}
#: {{co|en|'''gratuitous''' violence}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1983|writer=Lorenzo Semple Jr.|title={{w|Never Say Never Again}}|role={{w|Q (James Bond)|Q}}|actor=Alec McCowen|passage=Good to see you Mr. Bond. Things've been awfully dull 'round here. {{...|Bureaucrats running the whole place. Everything done by the book. Can't make a decision unless the computer gives you the go ahead.}} Now you're on this. I hope we're going to have some '''gratuitous''' sex and violence!}}

